  {
    "episode_id": 45336,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45336.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Well, yeah, first of all, we need to get people talking about federalism and separation of powers because that's what we lost the last time the Democrats tried to pack the court. When we get people talking about that, we make it part of the National Political Discourse. You shouldn't have anyone running, especially as a Republican, but really from either party, for the US House, or the US Senate, or the presidency of the United States. Without them talking about where they stand on how to restore federalism, the vertical protection that keeps most of the power at the state and local level, and how to restore separation of powers. The horizontal protection that says we're going to have one branch of government that makes the laws, Congress, the legislative branch, one branch of government headed by the president, the executive branch that enforces the laws, and another branch that interprets the laws headed by the Supreme Court. Those need to become mainstream political conversation topics in America, again, especially for federal races. And that is really how we fix this. But in the meantime, we've got to also counteract the clear and present threat that we face due to this now trending hashtag of hashtag expand the court pioneered by Elizabeth Warren and others who are trying overtly to attack the independence of the federal judiciary.",
