  {
    "episode_id": 45336,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/5/45336.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "I make no mystery about the fact that when there is a Democratic president in office, I understand that we're not going to get the same nominees as we would when there's a Republican holding the office of the presidency. It still matters and it still matters to me to review each candidate individually and figure out whether he or she understands, respects, appreciates, and will defiantly protect the independence of the judiciary and will understand that each case is to be decided on the basis of its own merits and on the basis of what the law says. Now, on the basis of the law, as it was understood at the time of an, it's an accident, if a statute, or at the time of its ratification, if it's a provision of the Constitution. And so I'll vote yes for a nominee, even if that nominee doesn't share my political worldview, if I think that nominee is sufficiently committed to the judicial task, if not, I won't. So I don't know that that ought to ever mean blind deference to any president, whether that president is of your own political party or not. It certainly doesn't mean that to me, and I don't think it should be anyone else either.",
